peak\n50\t      differently.\n51\t  - term: command\n52\t    means: >-\n53\t      One command line a user types to run the greeter, its arguments\n54\t      included. Everything a user sees of the greeter follows from one.\n55\t  - term: exit code\n56\t    means: >-\n57\t      The number the command ends with: 0 when it succeeded, anything\n58\t      else when it did not. Scripts calling the greeter branch on it.\n59\t  - term: standard output\n60\t    means: >-\n61\t      The lines the command prints for its user, its answer and its log\n62\t      records alike. It is the greeter's only visible output.\n63\t\n64\tcatalogue:\n65\t  - template: 'the greeting is configured as \"{greeting}\"'\n66\t    block: given\n67\t    means: >-\n68\t      The project's config file sets the greeting, the word the greeter\n69\t      prints before a name, to the quoted text.\n70\t    terms: [greeting]\n71\t  - template: 'the {actor:role} runs \"{command}\"'\n72\t    block: when\n73\t    means: >-\n74\t      That role runs the quoted command line once, in the project\n75\t      directory, and waits for it to exit.\n76\t    terms: [command]\n77\t  - template: 'the command exits with code {code:number}'\n78\t    block: then\n79\t    means: >-\n80\t      The command that role ran ended with that exit code; 0 means it\n81\t      succeeded.\n82\t    terms: [command, exit code]\n83\t  - template: 'stdout matches {expected:pattern}'\n84\t    block: then\n85\t    means: >-\n86\t      A line the command printed to its standard output matches the\n87\t      pattern, a regular expression: either the answer line the command\n88\t      prints for its user, or a log record's message or fields.\n89\t    terms: [command, standard output]\n90\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e6f25c81-d0ae-4e12-a0c5-90f2287bdcf5","uuid":"9105d9df-06cb-4429-988f-909a21a97c36","timestamp":"2026-10-25T11:35:45.246Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"{{CWD}}/docs/product/product.yaml","content":"# Product document for the greeter, the host project of the scen-check\n# fixtures that walk the shipped checklist.\n#\n# Its catalogue phrases every step of the claimed Scenario, and its\n# glossary defines every term those phrases declare; the architecture\n# document maps each of them. The vocabulary is what the quality\n# prompts rule against.\n\ntitle: \"Greeter\"\n\ndescription:\n  summary: >-\n    A command-line greeter: given a name, it prints the greeting its\n    config file sets, followed by that name.\n\nroles:\n  - name: Operator\n    definition: Runs the greeter from a terminal and reads what it prints.\n\nvocabulary:\n  # Vague words that must not appear in a scenario description or step.\n  forbidden_qualifiers:\n    - properly\n    - correctly\n    - appropriate\n    - appropriately\n    - user-friendly\n    - fast\n    - quickly\n    - helpful\n\n  # Action verbs too abstract to automate: they hide the interaction\n  # mechanism.\n  forbidden_actions:\n    - name: \"handle\"\n      reason: \"Hides what the program observably does with the input.\"\n      replacement: \"Name the observable action: prints, exits, writes.\"\n    - name: \"process\"\n      reason: \"Does not specify the mechanism or the visible outcome.\"\n      replacement: \"Name the observable action: prints, exits, writes.\"\n    - name: \"parse\"\n      reason: \"Describes internal mechanics, not observable behaviour.\"\n      replacement: \"Name what the user sees: prints, exits.\"\n\nglossary:\n  - term: greeting\n    means: >-\n      The word the greeter prints before a name, read from the project's\n      config file. It is what a user changes to make the greeter speak\n      differently.\n  - term: command\n    means: >-\n      One command line a user types to run the greeter, its arguments\n      included. Everything a user sees of the greeter follows from one.\n  - term: exit code\n    means: >-\n      The number the command ends with: 0 when it succeeded, anything\n  